<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Plain HTTP is allowed, because the servers this app talks to are the user's
    own and most of them live at http://192.168.x.x:8790 with no certificate to
    speak of. Since Android 9 that is blocked by default, and the block lands at
    exactly the worst moment: the pairing request fails with a policy error that
    reads like the server is down.

    There is no narrower rule available. The config file can only exempt named
    domains, and the whole premise here is that the address is supplied by the
    user at pair time — it is not known when this file is written.

    HTTPS is still the right answer the moment there is a reverse proxy or a
    tunnel in front of the server: the pairing claim and the device token both
    cross this connection, and on a shared network cleartext means anyone on it
    can read them. Nothing here prefers HTTP — it only stops the platform from
    refusing it.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
